WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.819 --> 00:00:02.995
Hi, this is Briana from freeCodeCamp

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.480
and today we're going to talk a little bit about

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:05.360
binary code.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.980
Now binary code is a really really basic simple

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.820
computer language where there's just

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.640
1's and there's 0's.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.460
There's bi (two things), 1's, and 0's.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:17.460
1's meaning on.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:18.630
0's meaning off.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.460
So think of like a light switch, which in some cases

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:23.500
super old computers there was an actual switch

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:24.600
which was either on or off.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.900
1 being on. 0 being off.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.080
Bunch of 1's and 0's. They can mean pretty much

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.288
anything, but really they're mostly meant to be

00:00:32.288 --> 00:00:33.180
numbers and letters.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.040
We're going to go in a little bit on how you actually

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.930
decode binary or translate a number to binary code.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.860
But really as you guessed, you can usually just Google

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.080
binary translator and have that figured out.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.140
The most important part of this:

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:50.700
Do you understand the concept that even basic things

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.300
like 0's and 1's can translate into something

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.299
really really complex.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.660
And it is the basis for all the computer languages

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.860
and programs that exist today.

